In Open: after computing abs, `abs = canonicalLedgerPath(abs)` before registry lookup and file read. Note: error messages will then contain the canonical path instead of the user-supplied path. Is that acceptable? Tests check `kind` only, not message, per grep. But "保留现有真实路径、相对路径和旧版账本的使用方式" — behavior preserved; messages may change to canonical path. Hmm, but maybe safer to keep the user-facing abs path in error messages while keying the registry by canonical path? But Ledger.path is used for persistLocked and tryEvict. If we key registry by canonical, Ledger.path must be canonical for tryEvict to work. persistLocked writes to l.path — canonical path writes to the same file anyway.

For error messages, using canonical path in "no ledger at %s" is fine and arguably more accurate. I'll just canonicalize early.

Edge: On Open of nonexistent file via symlink dir: canonicalLedgerPath resolves parent → canonical path → ReadFile fails with NotExist → ErrNotInit. Good, no file created.

Corrupt ledger via symlink: EvalSymlinks succeeds (file exists) → canonical → read → ErrCorrupt. Good.

CreateLedger: canonicalize too. `os.Stat(abs)` on canonical path — same file. Registry check with canonical key. atomicWrite to canonical path — writes through to the real location. Good. Also the case: CreateLedger via symlink path where file already exists via real path → stat canonical → ErrExists. Good.

One subtlety: CreateLedger's atomicWrite does MkdirAll(dir) — dir of canonical path exists already since EvalSymlinks(parent) succeeded. Fine.

Another subtlety: two handles opened, one via real one via symlink — both share registry entry, refs=2. Close one → refs=1, no evict. In-flight ops protected by ops counter. Good — existing mechanism handles it.

Also concurrent submits from both handles serialize on reg.mu. The 70/40 example: both handles share state and mutex, so second sees updated balance → insufficient_balance. Good.

What about `Ledger.path` field — set to canonical abs. Close uses it for tryEvict — matches registry key. Good.

Also `SetFailHook` — per registry entry, shared. Fine.

Now also consider: `Open` when registry already has entry under canonical key — returns shared. But what if the ledger was created via CreateLedger (registered with refs=0) under canonical key, then Open via symlink → canonical key matches → refs++ → returns without reading file. Fine, state is the created one.

Potential issue: tryEvict deletes when refs==0 && ops==0. After CreateLedger, entry with refs=0, ops=0 stays forever until an Open/Close cycle evicts. Pre-existing behavior; not our concern.

Wait, one more: in Open, currently the file read happens while holding openMu. Keep that.

Also should I canonicalize in a way that handles the file itself being a symlink? Task says directory symlink only, but EvalSymlinks handles both. Fine.

Let me also check ledger_test.go for how Open paths are checked in messages... grep showed nothing for "no ledger at". Let me quickly check tests referencing path in error messages.

